1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
In this video, I will explain the application techniques of UV.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
First, let's talk about what is UV.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
UV refers to the two axes of 2D texture space.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The level of U and vertical, V, correspond to the x-axis and y-axis in the standard 2D sticker.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
UV is the surface details such as the color pattern of the 3D model surface.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
After opening and processing, it is presented in the form of a 2D image.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
UV can tell the software how to wrap the 2D image on the surface of the 3D model.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
For example, the model of Santa Claus in the picture.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
On the left is the flat opening picture of the model surface and the UV picture of Santa Claus.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
The details such as color patterns and other surface details will be displayed in the corresponding position of the 3D model.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
When I open the UV image, I can see that the color pattern of the 3D model is in the 3D model.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:10,000
When we finish 3D clothing and want to import it into other 3D software, we need to process the UV image to ensure that the texture of the surface material can be correctly imported into other software.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Next, we open a package of the project file.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Open the UV editor in the main menu bar of the material UV.

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Since this package is made up of 2D flat panels, its UV image is the various panels of the 3D package.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
We can adjust the position of the panels by moving the slider to adjust the position of the panels.

17
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
In the right side of the UV editor is a series of tools for UV.

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
First, we fill in the UV image.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Click on the automatic UV fill.

20
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Open the window.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
In the window, first look at the UV area.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Here, the default option is to fill in the 0, 1 area.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Click on the application.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
All UVs will be displayed in the 0, 1 area.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,000
That is, in the first unit area of the first line of the coordinate axis.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The selected area can be self-defined and selected to fill in the coordinate area.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Here, we can use this option to arrange the UV image of the component separately in another area.

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Select the UV image of the component.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
The selected area is 1, 2.

30
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Click on the application.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
This way, the UV image of the component can be filled in the 1, 2 area.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Select the UV again.

33
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
In this way, the UV image of the component can be filled in the 0, 1 area.

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
This way, you can enlarge the accessories and the UV version to a certain extent.

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The clarity of the text is improved.

36
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Because most of the 3D application software, the only UV space that can be used is 0 to 1 coordinate space.

37
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
In order to better integrate with most 3D software, it is recommended to fill in the 0 to 1 coordinate area.

38
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
In this case, all UVs need to be filled in the 0, 1 area again.

39
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Next, let's talk about the meaning of these options.

40
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
The first one is fill in.

41
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Through it, the coordinate area of the UV image can be set.

42
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
The value range is 0.001 to 0.999.

43
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The larger the value, the greater the coordinate area between the panels.

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
For example, when I change the value to 0.050, you can see that the coordinate area of the panel UV is larger.

45
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
The corresponding UV image is also reduced.

46
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
We will change it to 0.001.

47
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Next, look at the accuracy.

48
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
The value can be adjusted within the range of 1 to 5.

49
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The larger the value, the more accurate the layout is placed.

50
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
To ensure the accuracy of the UV layout and more reasonable use of UV space,

51
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
At the same time, it also requires the appropriate calculation speed.

52
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The default value is 3.

53
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
But when the number of panels is too many and the layout speed is slower,

54
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
The accuracy can be reduced to produce a quick layout result.

55
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Next is the fixed ratio.

56
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Due to the relative size of the UV panel,

57
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
The default is the same as the 2D panel.

58
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The size ratio of the current size of the UV image that should be selected can be locked.

59
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
To maintain the ratio consistent with the 2D panel during the filling process.

60
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Therefore, the fixed ratio will generally be selected.

61
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
If the fixed ratio is cancelled and then filled,

62
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
You can see that in order to increase the usage rate of UV space,

63
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Some panels will be enlarged or reduced.

64
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
This will lead to the final 3D model.

65
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
There will be a difference in the degree of clarity between the different panels.

66
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Ctrl plus Z returns to the previous UV layout state.

67
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Restore the normal ratio of the panel or press the right button in the blank area directly.

68
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Select all UVs to be rearranged to the position of the 2D panel window.

69
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
This will make the UV and the 2D panel layout different.

70
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Select the fixed ratio.

71
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
The following is the rotation.

72
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Select Rotate and then click Apply.

73
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
You can see that in order to increase the usage rate of the UV space,

74
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The UV is rotated to some panels.

75
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
For UV images of patterns such as lines and flowers,

76
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
If you want to keep the pattern consistent in the number direction,

77
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
You don't have to select Rotate.

78
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
If the pattern of individual panels is not consistent in the direction,

79
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
You can right-click on the UV of the panel.

80
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Select the UV pattern with horizontal or vertical rotation to get a consistent UV pattern.

81
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
The following is the optimization layout.

82
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The optimization layout is a more advanced calculation method.

83
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It maximizes the usage rate of UV space.

84
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
In order to obtain a clearer and higher quality UV layout.

85
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
And reduce space waste to the maximum extent.

86
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
This will make the final pattern as clear as possible.

87
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
In contrast, the optimization layout will take a longer time to calculate.

88
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Here we choose the optimization layout.

89
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
There is one more thing to note.

90
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
The leather fabric used in this bag.

91
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
The back and sides use different materials and colors on the front and sides.

92
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
So how to show the pattern of the back and sides.

93
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And do the UV layout together?

94
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
First, select the pattern of the leather fabric used in the application.

95
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
At the bottom of the property editor.

96
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Find UV Expansion.

97
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Open the back and sides.

98
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Select the moving side of the pattern again.

99
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
You can see the UV pattern of the back and sides is displayed.

100
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
If the UV pattern of the back and sides is not displayed.

101
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
You need to check if the surface of the dense pattern is opened.

102
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Come to the UV fill window.

103
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
After the UV fill window options are set.

104
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Click on the application.

105
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
The UV pattern layout is completed.

106
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
You can apply the UV quick photo below.

107
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
The current UV layout is exported as a 2D image.

108
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
As a trial reference.

109
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Next is the baking pattern.

110
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
It can be according to the current UV layout.

111
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
The corresponding pattern, line drawing, calligraphy drawing, etc.

112
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
The different materials are baked and stored together.

113
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Before that.

114
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Before that.

115
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
You can check all the patterns through the top left corner.

116
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Open the baking pattern.

117
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Set up the preservation path and size pixels.

118
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
The following fill pattern opening and closing.

119
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
You can extend the pattern to the UV edge.

120
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Self-defined pixels or unlimited extension.

121
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Prevent the final pattern.

122
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,000
There is a visible gap as shown in the picture below.

123
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
The baking pattern is moving under the UV.

124
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
We select the plate that needs to be moved.

125
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Click on the UV move.

126
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
You can move the plate up and down or left and right.

127
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
The overall position is moved.

128
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
At the bottom distance here.

129
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
You can enter the unit distance that is moved once per unit.

130
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Here is 1.

131
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Move a unit area every time.

132
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Enter 0.5.

133
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Move 0.5 units area every time.

134
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
The tool is generally used to adjust the position of the overall UV pattern.

135
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
After processing the UV pattern of the 3D bag.

136
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Move it to another 3D format.

137
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
This process will also move the UV pattern of the file out.

138
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:51,000
For example, this time I need to move the file to the FBX format.

139
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
In the window of the spring, I chose a single target.

140
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
A single target can merge all the plates into a complete grid and move out.

141
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And multiple targets is to move each plate out as a separate grid.

142
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Then select the one after moving out.

143
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
The thin one only outputs the flat grid.

144
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
The thick one will contain the thickness of the fabric.

145
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So here I choose the thick one.

146
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Next, make sure to click on the unified UV coordinates.

147
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
In this way, all the UV information of the 3D bag can be combined into a single and orderly UV sticker.

148
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Ensure that the whole bag's pattern is correct and even.

149
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
After clicking on the unified UV coordinates, there will be some options below.

150
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Let's take a look at them separately.

151
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
The first one is the picture size.

152
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
It can be controlled.

153
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
It can control the size of the UV pattern.

154
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Generally, the number of inputs is 1024.

155
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,000
The larger the number, the clearer the FBX pattern will be obtained.

156
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
But the corresponding model size will also be larger.

157
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
The second one is the fill-in pattern.

158
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,000
The default is 5 pixels.

159
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
What does it do?

160
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Let's take a look at the following example.

161
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
In the new colo program, we import an FBX.

162
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
The fill-in pattern of this FBX is 0.

163
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
This means that the fill-in pattern does not extend beyond the edge.

164
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
We can see that the edge plus the part of the partition will appear illiterate.

165
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
This is because the printed version is thick.

166
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
According to the concept of UV.

167
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:31,000
The fill-in pattern of the partition is to extend to a certain distance,

168
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
in order to ensure that the part with a certain thickness is fully wrapped around the fill-in pattern.

169
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Here I set the fill texture to 8

170
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,000
It means that the edge of the UV image will have 8 pixel texture fill

171
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
This can ensure that the entire model is covered with texture

172
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Next we will import another FBX

173
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
This FBX is set to 8 pixel texture fill

174
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
After enlarging, you can see that the edge is normal

175
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Note

176
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
In order to prevent the UVs from overlapping after the texture fill

177
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
It is recommended to slightly enlarge the edge of the UV image when doing UV arrangement

178
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
For example, input 0.005 here in the fill

179
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
So that there will be a little more space between the sheets

180
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Provide sufficient space for the fill texture fill

181
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
After setting the fill texture fill

182
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Select the type of sticker that needs to be saved below

183
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Click to confirm that FBX has been exported

184
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
You can edit the exported FBX into other 3D software

185
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Here we can edit the import

186
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Due to the export model, the UV image is PNG

187
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
So the imported model will be a transparent effect

188
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,000
At this time, you need to open the high-quality rendering and you can check it normally

189
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Its UV image can be imported into a picture software

190
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
Edit in PSAI

191
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
For example, I made a color adjustment to the UV image

192
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
You can apply the edited UV image to the text in this column

193
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,000
This can make the FBX

194
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
This can make the FBX model more colorful

